WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:04.900
Tension pays for area.

2
00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:07.220
But how does force reach the channel?

3
00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:10.280
Through lipid, through a protein tether, or both?

4
00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:13.980
The idea is the work done by membrane tension.

5
00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:18.180
A tether could change that tension, or pull on the channel directly.

6
00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:20.440
That would add its own work term.

7
00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:23.280
The lipid route has a clean test.

8
00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:25.280
Take everything else away.

9
00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:31.830
Purified mouse Piezo1 was placed in droplet bilayers, with no other cellular

10
00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:32.830
components.

11
00:00:33.483 --> 00:00:37.103
A droplet bilayer forms where two lipid-coated droplets touch.

12
00:00:38.023 --> 00:00:39.423
Nothing from a cell is present.

13
00:00:40.433 --> 00:00:43.513
Suppose a tether were strictly required for opening.

14
00:00:44.373 --> 00:00:46.053
What should the purified channel have done?

15
00:00:46.873 --> 00:00:48.753
And what would its opening tell you about a cell?

16
00:00:54.767 --> 00:00:56.127
It should have stayed silent.

17
00:00:57.007 --> 00:01:00.887
Yet force still activated it, so protein plus lipid is sufficient there.

18
00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:09.170
But that says nothing about whether an intact cell also uses a second route.

19
00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:12.010
A second route has been proposed.

20
00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:17.110
Wang and colleagues described a chain of cadherin, beta-catenin and actin.

21
00:01:18.050 --> 00:01:23.290
Disrupting the channel's link to E-cadherin prevented cytoskeleton-dependent gating

22
00:01:23.290 --> 00:01:24.350
in their cultured cells.

23
00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:26.940
These need not be rivals.

24
00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:31.460
Sufficiency in one preparation cannot exclude a route in another.

25
00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:33.980
That is why the field has not chosen.

26
00:01:35.033 --> 00:01:37.933
There is even a hint that the bilayer misses something.

27
00:01:38.673 --> 00:01:42.793
The purified channel lacked the rapid inactivation seen in whole cells.

28
00:01:43.867 --> 00:01:47.587
Inactivation is where human genetics meets this biophysics.

29
00:01:48.587 --> 00:01:51.407
Distal arthrogryposis is an inherited condition.

30
00:01:52.487 --> 00:01:55.827
Joints of the hands and feet are fixed in bent positions from birth.

31
00:01:56.850 --> 00:02:02.070
Two Piezo2 variants from such families were tested in a human cell line.

32
00:02:02.930 --> 00:02:04.750
Both sped recovery from inactivation.

33
00:02:05.790 --> 00:02:08.070
Only one slowed inactivation during the pulse.

34
00:02:09.083 --> 00:02:12.723
So slower inactivation is not a rule for every variant.

35
00:02:13.663 --> 00:02:14.663
Read each one.

36
00:02:15.383 --> 00:02:18.503
Loss of function looks different in mice and in people.

37
00:02:19.603 --> 00:02:24.923
Adult mice lacking Piezo2 in sensory neurons and Merkel cells lose almost all

38
00:02:24.923 --> 00:02:25.923
light-touch behaviour.

39
00:02:26.917 --> 00:02:31.437
Two people without working Piezo2 had impaired discriminative touch.

40
00:02:32.397 --> 00:02:36.517
Their sense of limb position was profoundly impaired, and worse without vision.

41
00:02:38.417 --> 00:02:39.997
But they kept other abilities.

42
00:02:40.997 --> 00:02:42.237
Their deficits were selective.

43
00:02:43.157 --> 00:02:46.077
Keep the mouse sentences and the human sentences apart.

44
00:02:47.117 --> 00:02:48.737
Now back to the first question.

45
00:02:49.697 --> 00:02:52.977
Why does one channel need 114 helices?

46
00:02:53.797 --> 00:02:56.477
In the leading model, still debated, to collect area.

47
00:02:58.150 --> 00:02:59.650
Tension pays for area.

48
00:03:00.550 --> 00:03:03.370
Whether a tether adds its own force is a separate question.
